<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Myitnge River</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Dokhtawady, Dokhtawaddy River, Namtu River</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Myitnge, or Dokhtawady, is a river of central Myanmar that rises in the Shan Hills and flows west to join the Irrawaddy just south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandalay">Mandalay</a>. At the confluence stood Ava, or Inwa, capital of Burmese kingdoms for long periods between 1365 and 1842, whose site was protected by the two rivers and a canal.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/myitnge-river.jpg" alt="Myitnge River" width="320" height="218" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Wagaung · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:River_Myitnge.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
